  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "[Demonstrating how it can be used to update a probability]\n\nBob draws a card at random from a standard deck of cards and places it face-down in front of him. He bets that the card he drew was a king.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What is the probability that the card drawn is a king?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{13}$",
          "worked": "There are four kings among the 52 equally likely cards.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{King}) &= \\frac{4}{52}\\\\P(\\text{King}) &= \\frac{1}{13}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p31-q2-b-information",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Mary, who is secretly helping Bob, sees that the card he placed down was a picture card. She tells Bob, “You drew a picture card, but I did not see which one it was.”",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p31-q2-b-given",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Explain intuitively why the probability that Bob drew a king is now ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac{1}{3}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Four of the 12 equally likely picture cards are kings.",
          "worked": "Mary’s information restricts the possible cards to the four jacks, four queens and four kings. Each of these 12 cards is equally likely, so four of the 12 possibilities are kings. That gives $\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p31-q2-c-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The following is the formal calculation of the same problem. Complete the calculation and verify your answer from (b).",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p31-q2-c-scaffold",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(\\text{King}\\mid\\text{Picture})=\\frac{P(\\text{King})\\times P(\\text{Picture}\\mid\\text{King})}{P(\\text{Picture})}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p31-q2-c-hint",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hint: ",
                  "marks": [
                    "bold"
                  ],
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Think carefully about what ",
                  "marks": [],
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(\\text{Picture}\\mid\\text{King})",
                  "display": false,
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " means.",
                  "marks": [],
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$. Every king is a picture card.\n\n$P(\\text{Picture}\\mid\\text{King})=1$.",
          "worked": "Every king is a picture card, so $P(\\text{Picture}\\mid\\text{King})=1$. There are 12 picture cards, so $P(\\text{Picture})=\\frac{12}{52}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{King}\\mid\\text{Picture}) &= \\frac{P(\\text{King})\\times P(\\text{Picture}\\mid\\text{King})}{P(\\text{Picture})}\\\\P(\\text{King}\\mid\\text{Picture}) &= \\frac{\\frac{4}{52}\\times 1}{\\frac{12}{52}}\\\\P(\\text{King}\\mid\\text{Picture}) &= \\frac{4}{12}\\\\P(\\text{King}\\mid\\text{Picture}) &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis agrees with the probability in part (b).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
